sec.p:
Our implementation of the Getis-Ord score for protein three-dimensional structure was inspired by PIVOTAL, which applied the Getis-Ord score to clustering of mutations in human disease.45–47 The two values input to the Getis-Ord statistic computation are a per-residue score x, here the per-amino acid homoplasy score, and a weight matrix W that contains the inverse of the inter-residue distances computed from the wild-type amino acids. x is an L x 1 vector where the entry x! contains the homoplasy score for the i-th residue in the protein, and W is an L x L matrix with entries defined as:
wi,j={1di,jifi≠j0ifi=j \begin{document}$$\displaystyle w_{i, j}=\left\{\begin{array}{c}\frac{1}{d_{i, j}} \text { if } i \neq j \\ 0 \text { if } i=j\end{array}\right.$$\end{document}
Where di,j is the minimum inter-residue atomic distance, in Å, between i and j. Thus, residues that are closer in three dimensions will have higher weights.

The Getis-Ord statistic for residue i is calculated as:


Gi=∑j=1Lwi,jxj−X¯∑j=1Lwi,jSK
\begin{document}$$\displaystyle G_{i}=\frac{\sum_{j=1}^{L} w_{i, j} x_{j}-\bar{X} \sum_{j=1}^{L} w_{i, j}}{S K}$$\end{document}



Where X̅ is the mean of x, and:


S=∑j=1Lxj2L−X¯2K=L∑j=1Lwi,j2−(∑j=1Lwi,j)2L−1
\begin{document}$$\displaystyle \begin{aligned} & S=\sqrt{\frac{\sum_{j=1}^{L} x_{j}^{2}}{L}-\bar{X}^{2}} \\ & K=\sqrt{\frac{L \sum_{j=1}^{L} w_{i, j}^{2}-\left(\sum_{j=1}^{L} w_{i, j}\right)^{2}}{L-1}}\end{aligned}$$\end{document}
